7592186045417] [17592186083936 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186082547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083936 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79267" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083936 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083936 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the range of the function $h$ defined by the following table?\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $3$ | $-4$ | $5$ | $-6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $h(x)$ | $-7$ | $6$ | $-5$ | $4$ | $-3$ | $6$ | $-1$ | $0$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083936 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e24a0352-55e7-51e0-b32f-99eea47a5c5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083936 #edb/ref 1074 "The range of $h(x)$ consists of all the outputs that have a corresponding $x$-input.\n\nIn a table, the range consists of all numbers that feature in the row corresponding to $h(x).$\n\nTherefore, the range is the set $\\{-7,-5,-3,-1,0,4,6\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084040 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084040 #edb/ref 1061 150950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084040 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2642b03f-30f8-5a73-8a08-f51f7b592baf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084040 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084041 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084041 #edb/ref 1061 150951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084041 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4f14b6dd-48cf-50bf-9faa-ac12c6ebb738" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084041 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084042 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084042 #edb/ref 1061 150954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084042 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5f681af9-5dd3-5ccc-8663-eb8759acc874" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084042 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084043 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084043 #edb/ref 1061 150957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084043 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0e0d7002-1126-53ab-8ffd-a09dbe4a234a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084043 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084044 #edb/ref 1061 150958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084044 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5bd906c2-2b91-5274-869f-0425ba950919" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084044 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084518 #edb/ref 1064 "The **range** of a function is the collection of numbers (or set) containing all the function's outputs.\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$, defined by the mapping diagram below.\n\n![](images/60/60ae5361f9f51bb7e61083269770ee3dd432e04ad2f968df07d78c8dc61e7188.png)\n\nIn a mapping diagram, the range consists of all the outputs to which an arrow is drawn. Therefore, the range is\n\n$$\n\\{-1,2,3 \\}\n$$\n\nThe value $y=0$ is not part of the range because there is no $x$-value such that\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084518 #edb/ref 1065 8799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084518 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a6969f16-6b56-552e-8162-ba459b815dc6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084518 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084519 #edb/ref 1064 "When we graph a function, the range is the set of all $y$-values given by the graph.\n\nFor example, consider the linear function\n\n$$\nf(x) = 2x\n$$\n\ngraphed below.\n\n![](images/09/09716693ba950bd3d14d0ea008dbd9d99dc51a26f30e87ccbf1559f34329fed9.png)\n\nThe graph covers all $y$-values, so the range of the function is\n\n$$\n-\\infty < y< \\infty\n$$\n\nwhich we can also write as\n$-\\infty < f(x) < \\infty$.\n\nFurthermore, we can express the range as $f(x)\\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$ using interval notation.\n\nIf we restrict the domain, the range also changes. Let's now consider the function\n\n$$\ng(x) = 2x, \\quad x \\in [0,2)\n$$\n\nThe graph $y = g(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/79/791aca2b232c0eee5950c4c1c87073b4c51cd5d61d094de893ca4a2ec28e8d1e.png)\n\nThe $y$ values go from $y=0$ up to (but not including) $y=4$, including every value in between. Therefore, the range of the function is\n\n$$\n0\\leq g(x) < 4\n$$\n\nUsing interval notation, we can also write the range as $g(x)\\in [0,4)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084519 #edb/ref 1065 8801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084519 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "35906cd0-d08e-5f2a-b88e-9e8ac1329502" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084519 #edb/ref 1067 "Graphs of Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487556 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186488287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487556 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186488288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487556 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186488318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487556 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186488326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487556 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186488343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487556 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487556 #edb/ref 1045 "The Range of a Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487556 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186488318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487556 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487556 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487556 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "4dc16135-b9af-5c1e-ab88-b2de2e7d8742" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487575 #edb/ref 1080 "<" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487575 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487575 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "049ea557-4d92-5f5b-878c-bb4d41b99074" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487595 #edb/ref 1080 "0<f(x)<3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487595 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487595 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "10b99c2c-7707-5d66-8da2-a5686ff27235" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487599 #edb/ref 1080 "[-6,7]" #edb/ref 1759